WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Kelly Edwards, Staff Writer, Author, Producer:&nbsp;And&nbsp;
my daughter calls me and we had this conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:10.720
and I asked her I said&nbsp;Lily, I think I might want&nbsp;
to give this a shot&nbsp;and she said,&nbsp;oh wow, okay.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:17.200
Film Courage: Kelly, if you were given a&nbsp;
choice between working as an executive for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.280
$100,000 a year or working as a screenwriter&nbsp;
for $25,000 a year, which would you choose?&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:34.240
Kelly:&nbsp;First of all executives make a lot&nbsp;
more than $100,000 a year. I actually had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:45.040
make that decision at the top of 2020 when I was&nbsp;
offered a promotion at HBO and I was going to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:52.640
a $100,000 more than I made currently and I turned&nbsp;
it down because also they made me an offer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.160
take a first look writing deal and I jumped at it&nbsp;
and it was probably half of what I was making. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.600
I’ve actually had this question in real time,&nbsp;
in real life and there’s nothing like it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.960
think if I went back and had to do it all&nbsp;
over again I would make the same choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:18.640
There’s just nothing like waking up every day&nbsp;
in pure joy and knowing that the first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.840
that I get to do is crack open my computer and&nbsp;
the last thing I’m doing all day is still being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.360
wired into that computer, just downloading&nbsp;
everything that’s in my head. There’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.480
getting paid to write there’s just no question.&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.720
Film Courage: Do you think you would have&nbsp;
really taken that? I know you had some jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.680
out of college where you were an administrative&nbsp;
assistant or helping agents things like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:53.840
but the lure of six figures or more, do you think&nbsp;
it takes time to realize what is important?&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.240
Kelly:&nbsp;Let me see if I understand your&nbsp;
question, if you’re saying did it take me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.960
30 years to know that I wanted to be a writer?&nbsp;
I don’t think I was consciously aware in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:15.200
of that time that I was an executive that I was&nbsp;
planning on making a transition. I think I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.560
started out as a writer's assistant when I was you&nbsp;
know just coming out of college it was one of my I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.000
think it was my third job that I'd ever had in the&nbsp;
entertainment business and I knew that I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.600
to do that I wanted to be a part of the creative&nbsp;
process and I tried casting and I knew that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.080
wasn't really it even though I loved the process&nbsp;
I love the people I was working with and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.720
I was looking for my place and I it felt right&nbsp;
because I'd always been writing I'd been writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.000
you know since I was a kid you know creating&nbsp;
short plays and short stories and you know doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.360
all those things actually creating a magazine so&nbsp;
for my family so I knew that I wanted to somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.720
channel all that into writing but when I was&nbsp;
coming out of college the world was very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.960
than it is now there just weren't a lot of places&nbsp;
for an African-American young woman who wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.800
write in in the ecosystem and when I looked around&nbsp;
I didn't see a lot of people who looked like me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.840
either in either in executive ranks or as a as&nbsp;
a writer on staff of a show so I felt as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.960
there were not a whole lot of doors that were open&nbsp;
for me there were not a lot of places that I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.440
go so I think if if the world had been different&nbsp;
if I had come out now as a writer if I was doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.200
that you know when I was 20 something years old&nbsp;
and doing it now boy sky's the limit but at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.360
time I made the best choice that I could with the&nbsp;
information that I could and with the knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.120
that I needed to make a living and that there were&nbsp;
not going to be a lot of opportunities for me so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.880
so I do feel as though going into as an executive&nbsp;
route was the perfect place for me because I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:01.680
still work with the creative I could still be a&nbsp;
part of that writing process only from the hey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.880
let me help you you know let's talk about your&nbsp;
project let's figure out where you want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.840
and how to get there and and the packaging part&nbsp;
that you do as you're in a network executive or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.240
as a feature executive so I do feel as&nbsp;
though it really satisfied a lot of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.960
cravings of working with material&nbsp;
and I think I was really good at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.640
and I got a chance to see the industry from a very&nbsp;
very different perspective than a lot of writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.800
do so I saw the in inner workings and realized oh&nbsp;
there are so many different decisions that get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.360
you know that are part of the of the creative&nbsp;
process that are on the business side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
that you don't see as a writer you only know&nbsp;
the work that you've put out but you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.640
know all the other things that make into the&nbsp;
decision-making process of why this thing moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.880
forward versus something else that doesn't move&nbsp;
forward and then once it does move forward well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.360
what else do you have to do in order to support&nbsp;
that piece of material you know from the marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.360
and and advertising and all of the other aspects&nbsp;
of it so I think I got a really great insider view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.280
into the business on both the feature side and&nbsp;
the tv side that I probably wouldn't have had so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.880
I kind of feel like I got baked in a different way&nbsp;
I was always writing though and I think that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:20.800
that that need for me to express myself on the&nbsp;
page was always there so I don't think you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.760
go through 30 years of you know how I did it that&nbsp;
was just a kind of a a weird way to you know for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.720
me to do it but I know that there are there's&nbsp;
also a very long legacy of of executives who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:39.360
turned writers I don't know that they talk about&nbsp;
it but but it's not like I blazed a whole new path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.960
and I do feel like you know I sort of&nbsp;
got to the right place at the right time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.840
okay so maybe not the 30-year journey maybe&nbsp;
if you were to give advice to someone on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.680
a paycheck versus feeling creatively fulfilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.800
well I think that's always the balance isn't it&nbsp;
you know I was actually just talking to a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:07.200
of people recently about that you know art versus&nbsp;
commerce and how much you know can you survive on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.600
while you were fulfilling your dream and how and&nbsp;
what does success look like to you so you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.840
there might be writers who are are completely&nbsp;
content having you know doing plays in in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.520
regional theater you know and that's a success as&nbsp;
a writer so I think whatever you feel as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.000
your calling is in this it doesn't have to be that&nbsp;
you're winning an Oscar doesn't have to be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.560
you're making a marvel feature you know you can&nbsp;
write and be satisfied by that writing and create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.880
in so many different ways and yeah the money&nbsp;
thing is is it's hard it's harder when you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.040
older it's certainly harder if I did this when&nbsp;
I was 24 years old and right out of college and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:55.840
my I think my rent at the time this was back at&nbsp;
80 something my rent at the time was 450 a month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.800
that was it that's all I had to make to cover that&nbsp;
nut now it's like I got a mortgage I've got three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.360
kids there was college education you know there's&nbsp;
a lot more that you know there's there's car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.640
insurance there's homeowners and shares there's&nbsp;
so many things that just become so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.160
oppressive and you have to make different choices&nbsp;
based on what's coming in and what's not coming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:24.320
I have the luxury now of having an expertise&nbsp;
in something that people want that thing can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:30.560
support me as I also pursue this writing so if&nbsp;
it's not if it's a lean month you know i'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:36.240
something to fall back on that's the benefit&nbsp;
you know and I think also my age you know at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:43.760
at my age now there be there's a as I don't know&nbsp;
if it's it's like a feeling of being settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.160
and knowing that you have worth and you have&nbsp;
value that I don't think I had when I was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.240
my 20s in my 20s I thought oh it's going to&nbsp;
be my last job ever it was always the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.760
oh my god they're never going to hire me I'm&nbsp;
never going to work again you don't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.400
when you're in your 50s because you know you've&nbsp;
got something that you're bringing to the table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:08.080
what about peer pressure family pressure to make&nbsp;
money or maybe it's not even spoken it's just oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.640
hey it's a family get together and so and so they&nbsp;
just now their partner at a new firm and what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.040
you up to you know that kind of thing where you&nbsp;
know you feel like you have to explain yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:24.400
I think I have the benefit of coming from a family&nbsp;
that's incredibly supportive and my mother who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:30.400
is my biggest cheerleader and and every single&nbsp;
morning you know calls me up and says you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.520
calls me her precious child and just thinks that&nbsp;
I'm a genius so I think that that's that helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:43.360
and when I made this decision to to jump off the&nbsp;
edge of the cliff I happen I know exactly when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:48.800
was I could probably even pinpoint the day and the&nbsp;
moment because it was very very important to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:56.560
I happened to be at Sundance I was in the episodic&nbsp;
lab this is 2019. It was in October it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:06.240
the day before we were going to finish the program&nbsp;
and and it probably was a Saturday actually if I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:13.120
really being specific and I was in line to get&nbsp;
my to get dinner it was a buffet line and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:18.320
daughter calls me and we had this conversation&nbsp;
and I asked her I said I said Lily I think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:26.160
might want to give this a shot and she said&nbsp;
oh wow okay she said if not now mom when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.600
and for me I was like I birthed the&nbsp;
right child because I said look it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.440
we might actually end up having to have&nbsp;
you know make some choices financially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:48.400
because look I was coming off of a really nice&nbsp;
svp job at a major studio you know network and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:57.600
and she just said hey we'll be okay we'll figure&nbsp;
it out and at this point I think my my kids were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:05.200
I think I was probably I don't know I'm trying to&nbsp;
think if I just graduated from my MFA program or I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.040
think I had just graduated from my MFA program&nbsp;
and they my kids were still in school so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.560
were still you know it was their last year of&nbsp;
college and I think her last year of maybe of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:23.040
her graduate school and and again I feel like&nbsp;
there was just this understanding that we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:31.200
all figure it out together and that it wasn't that&nbsp;
important that the trappings of life the cars the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:36.800
house the whatever the vacations that we would&nbsp;
go on were just not that important compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:47.680
mom finding herself and I just couldn't have asked&nbsp;
for a better support system so I do feel as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:51.840
when you're when you're somebody who doesn't have&nbsp;
that when you have to really struggle and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:11:01.040
have to do that for yourself you do need to find&nbsp;
people who are going to acknowledge your dream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:06.720
and support your dream and to encourage your&nbsp;
dream because I think that a lot of writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.560
I think that's probably the biggest thing&nbsp;
that's the that's one of the biggest hurdles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:17.760
I think imposter syndrome and the the fact that&nbsp;
no one's in your corner saying hey keep going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.600
that's what makes us give up right&nbsp;
that's what makes us feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.840
you know maybe we're you know charging&nbsp;
in the wrong direction or we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.400
we're not on the right path because there are&nbsp;
all these pressures they're all these people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

